1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
This bottle of soda water has been sitting

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:03,680
in the fridge for a week, with the

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:05,120
cap screwed on tight.

4
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:06,960
Nothing about it changes.

5
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:10,000
The bubbles don't grow, the level doesn't move,

6
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,600
and if you checked tomorrow it would look

7
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:12,880
exactly the same.

8
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:16,000
So has everything inside it stopped?

9
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:18,080
Or is something still going on that we

10
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:19,120
just can't see?

11
00:00:20,334 --> 00:00:22,493
Let's zoom in to the surface, where the

12
00:00:22,574 --> 00:00:24,173
liquid meets the gas above it.

13
00:00:24,734 --> 00:00:27,213
The bottle holds dissolved carbon dioxide in the

14
00:00:27,294 --> 00:00:30,173
water, and carbon dioxide gas in the space

15
00:00:30,254 --> 00:00:31,093
at the top.

16
00:00:31,374 --> 00:00:34,973
Particles are always moving, so every second some

17
00:00:35,054 --> 00:00:37,693
dissolved molecules at the surface break free and

18
00:00:37,934 --> 00:00:39,053
escape into the gas.

19
00:00:39,454 --> 00:00:42,413
And every second, some gas molecules hit the

20
00:00:42,574 --> 00:00:44,013
water and dissolve back in.

21
00:00:44,574 --> 00:00:47,053
When the cap first went on, more escaped

22
00:00:47,134 --> 00:00:48,093
than returned.

23
00:00:48,334 --> 00:00:50,093
But as gas built up in the space

24
00:00:50,334 --> 00:00:52,653
above, more of it dived back in each

25
00:00:52,734 --> 00:00:55,133
second, until the return rate caught up with

26
00:00:55,214 --> 00:00:56,093
the escape rate.

27
00:00:56,494 --> 00:00:59,133
From then on, they match: for every molecule

28
00:00:59,214 --> 00:01:01,293
that leaves, another one comes back.

29
00:01:01,774 --> 00:01:04,093
The double arrow in this equation means both

30
00:01:04,334 --> 00:01:05,933
directions are happening at once.

31
00:01:07,134 --> 00:01:09,453
This is called dynamic equilibrium.

32
00:01:09,614 --> 00:01:12,813
Dynamic, because both processes keep going.

33
00:01:13,294 --> 00:01:16,893
Equilibrium, because their effects cancel out, so nothing

34
00:01:16,974 --> 00:01:18,013
changes overall.

35
00:01:18,654 --> 00:01:20,093
Here's a picture to hold onto.

36
00:01:20,654 --> 00:01:22,413
Think of the water and the gas as

37
00:01:22,494 --> 00:01:24,573
two towns, joined by a road at the

38
00:01:24,654 --> 00:01:27,133
surface, with molecules as the traffic.

39
00:01:27,774 --> 00:01:30,013
The same number of cars drive each way

40
00:01:30,174 --> 00:01:33,213
every minute, so the traffic never stops, but

41
00:01:33,334 --> 00:01:35,613
each town's population stays the same.

42
00:01:36,334 --> 00:01:39,693
Unlike drivers, molecules don't choose where to go,

43
00:01:39,734 --> 00:01:41,293
they just move about at random.

44
00:01:41,854 --> 00:01:43,533
And notice the towns don't have to be

45
00:01:43,614 --> 00:01:44,653
the same size.

46
00:01:45,054 --> 00:01:47,773
Equal rates, not equal amounts.

47
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:50,920
Now compare a campfire.

48
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:54,040
The wood burns, gives off heat, light and

49
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:57,240
gases, and eventually all that's left is cold

50
00:01:57,400 --> 00:01:58,080
ash.

51
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:00,160
That reaction has finished.

52
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:02,920
It can't run backwards: the ash won't turn

53
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:03,800
back into wood.

54
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:07,080
Nothing is happening in either direction any more,

55
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:08,920
so both rates are zero.

56
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:13,480
That's static equilibrium: no change, because nothing is

57
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:14,520
happening at all.

58
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,480
It's not dynamic equilibrium running slowly.

59
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:19,480
It has completely stopped.

60
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:21,980
Back to our soda water.

61
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:24,420
What happens if we take the cap off?

62
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:27,180
Carbon dioxide escaping from the water can now

63
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:29,420
drift out into the room, and the room

64
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:31,900
is huge, so very little of it ever

65
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:32,940
comes back.

66
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:36,300
Molecules keep leaving faster than they return, and

67
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:38,460
the drink slowly goes flat.

68
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:40,780
With the cap on, the bottle is a

69
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:44,220
closed system: matter can't get in or out.

70
00:02:44,700 --> 00:02:47,180
With the cap off, it's an open system.

71
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:51,020
That's why dynamic equilibrium needs a closed system.

72
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:53,500
If the returning half of the exchange keeps

73
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:56,540
getting lost, the two rates can never match.

74
00:02:57,767 --> 00:03:00,326
Let's use this to classify systems, the way

75
00:03:00,407 --> 00:03:02,006
an exam question might ask.

76
00:03:02,487 --> 00:03:04,406
Ask these questions in order.

77
00:03:04,727 --> 00:03:07,846
First: has the reaction finished, so it can't

78
00:03:07,927 --> 00:03:08,926
run backwards?

79
00:03:09,207 --> 00:03:11,046
If yes, it's static.

80
00:03:11,287 --> 00:03:13,446
If it can still go both ways, ask

81
00:03:13,527 --> 00:03:14,086
two more.

82
00:03:14,727 --> 00:03:16,486
Is the system closed?

83
00:03:16,607 --> 00:03:19,046
And are the things we can observe, like

84
00:03:19,127 --> 00:03:21,766
colour or concentration, staying constant?

85
00:03:22,327 --> 00:03:24,886
If both answers are yes, it's dynamic.

86
00:03:25,207 --> 00:03:26,926
If not, it's neither.

87
00:03:27,287 --> 00:03:29,366
Try it on a sealed flask of hydrogen

88
00:03:29,447 --> 00:03:32,406
and iodine gas, kept at 450 degrees for

89
00:03:32,567 --> 00:03:33,446
several hours.

90
00:03:33,927 --> 00:03:35,686
The purple colour isn't changing.

91
00:03:36,167 --> 00:03:37,766
Has the reaction finished?

92
00:03:37,847 --> 00:03:39,606
No, it can go both ways.

93
00:03:40,087 --> 00:03:41,366
Is it closed?

94
00:03:41,527 --> 00:03:42,566
Yes, it's sealed.

95
00:03:43,127 --> 00:03:44,566
Is the colour steady?

96
00:03:44,767 --> 00:03:45,166
Yes.

97
00:03:45,367 --> 00:03:47,446
So that's dynamic equilibrium.

98
00:03:48,667 --> 00:03:51,026
Now the cold ash from our campfire.

99
00:03:51,227 --> 00:03:52,906
Has the reaction finished?

100
00:03:53,107 --> 00:03:56,506
Yes, and it can't reverse, so it's static.

101
00:03:56,587 --> 00:03:58,746
We don't need the other questions, even though

102
00:03:58,827 --> 00:04:00,266
the ash is sitting out in the open

103
00:04:00,347 --> 00:04:01,226
air.

104
00:04:01,467 --> 00:04:04,346
Last, a beaker of water slowly evaporating in

105
00:04:04,427 --> 00:04:05,706
a warm room.

106
00:04:05,787 --> 00:04:08,746
Evaporation can go both ways, because some vapour

107
00:04:08,867 --> 00:04:12,106
does condense back in, so it hasn't finished.

108
00:04:12,147 --> 00:04:13,306
Is it closed?

109
00:04:13,427 --> 00:04:16,186
No, it's open, and far more water escapes

110
00:04:16,267 --> 00:04:19,146
than returns, so the level keeps dropping.

111
00:04:19,227 --> 00:04:20,506
That makes it neither.

112
00:04:21,734 --> 00:04:22,653
Your turn.

113
00:04:22,934 --> 00:04:25,333
Use the same questions to classify each of

114
00:04:25,414 --> 00:04:28,773
these as static, dynamic or neither, and give

115
00:04:28,854 --> 00:04:29,573
your reason.

116
00:04:30,294 --> 00:04:32,933
One: a sealed jar of salt water that

117
00:04:33,014 --> 00:04:34,853
has sat untouched for weeks.

118
00:04:35,334 --> 00:04:37,973
The water is saturated, which means it can't

119
00:04:38,054 --> 00:04:41,013
dissolve any more salt overall, and the pile

120
00:04:41,054 --> 00:04:43,333
of crystals at the bottom stays exactly the

121
00:04:43,414 --> 00:04:44,213
same size.

122
00:04:44,934 --> 00:04:47,493
Two: a cup of hot coffee cooling on

123
00:04:47,534 --> 00:04:48,213
a desk.

124
00:04:48,854 --> 00:04:51,573
Three: a strip of magnesium that has burned

125
00:04:51,654 --> 00:04:53,613
completely to white powder.

126
00:04:53,894 --> 00:04:55,893
Pause the video if you'd like more time.

127
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:23,140
The salt jar is dynamic.

128
00:05:23,540 --> 00:05:26,980
Dissolving can go both ways: salt keeps dissolving

129
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:30,180
from the pile, and dissolved salt keeps crystallising

130
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:32,420
back onto it, at the same rate, in

131
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:33,460
a closed jar.

132
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:36,580
That's why the pile stays the same size.

133
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:40,420
If you said static, that's the trap: still-looking

134
00:05:40,580 --> 00:05:41,860
doesn't mean stopped.

135
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:43,780
The coffee is neither.

136
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:46,100
The cup is open, and heat and steam

137
00:05:46,180 --> 00:05:48,340
keep leaving, so it's still changing.

138
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:50,740
It isn't at equilibrium at all.

139
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:53,380
And the burned magnesium is static.

140
00:05:53,780 --> 00:05:55,700
If you said neither because it's sitting in

141
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:58,420
open air, go back to the first question:

142
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:01,300
the reaction has finished and can't run backwards,

143
00:06:01,380 --> 00:06:02,420
so it's static.

144
00:06:03,634 --> 00:06:05,473
So here's the order to use in any

145
00:06:05,554 --> 00:06:06,513
question.

146
00:06:06,714 --> 00:06:09,393
First, has the reaction finished, so it can't

147
00:06:09,474 --> 00:06:10,673
run backwards?

148
00:06:10,874 --> 00:06:12,353
Then it's static.

149
00:06:12,394 --> 00:06:14,273
If it can still go both ways, and

150
00:06:14,354 --> 00:06:17,633
the system is closed and steady, it's dynamic.

151
00:06:17,714 --> 00:06:21,073
If it's open, or still changing, it's neither.

152
00:06:21,114 --> 00:06:24,033
And remember, at dynamic equilibrium the rates are

153
00:06:24,114 --> 00:06:26,353
equal, not the amounts.

154
00:06:26,434 --> 00:06:29,713
Next, we'll watch a reaction actually reach equilibrium,

155
00:06:29,834 --> 00:06:32,273
by following its two rates as they change.

156
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:34,860
Here are the key notes.

157
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:37,020
Pause the video if you need more time

158
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:37,900
to copy them.
